{"chapter":3,"deckId":"core-pure-1-c03-full","source":"CP1-Chp3-Series.pptx","sourceSha256":"33b392305d350de90fe788413c580dae961c76f05478987fbc8524d0b50d4439","width":1600,"height":900,"slides":[{"id":"core-pure-1-c03-full-s001","chapter":3,"sourceSlide":1,"title":"Series","shapes":[{"id":"native-1","kind":"sp","box":{"x":40,"y":35,"w":1520,"h":100},"fill":"transparent","line":{"color":"transparent"},"transcription":{"tex":"\\textbf{Core Pure 1}\\par Chapter 3: Series","fontSize":32,"hangingIndent":0,"color":"#151515"}}],"animation":{"steps":[],"triggers":{},"initialHidden":[]},"deckId":"core-pure-1-c03-full","courseId":"core-pure-1","variant":"full","covers":[],"headerShapes":[],"titlePageCandidate":true,"titlePage":false,"extension":false,"reviewStatus":"source-extracted-not-approved","contentWidth":1600,"contentHeight":850,"measuredLayout":true,"answerOrder":[],"nextActionOrder":[],"afterAnswerActions":{},"sourceMapping":{"method":"Native text and LaTeX conversion; acceptance tracked separately","sourceSlide":1}},{"id":"core-pure-1-c03-full-s002","chapter":3,"sourceSlide":2,"title":"Chapter Overview","shapes":[{"id":"native-1","kind":"sp","box":{"x":40,"y":35,"w":748,"h":565},"fill":"transparent","line":{"color":"transparent"},"transcription":{"tex":"A series is a sum of a finite or infinite number of terms. In Pure Year 1, \\(\\Sigma\\) (“capital sigma”) denotes summation.\\par What we will cover:\\par 1. First \\(n\\) integers: \\(\\sum_{r=1}^n r\\).\\par 2. First \\(n\\) squares: \\(\\sum_{r=1}^n r^2\\).\\par 3. First \\(n\\) cubes: \\(\\sum_{r=1}^n r^3\\).\\par 4. Breaking down complex sums: \\(\\sum_{r=1}^n(3-2r^2+4r)\\).\\par 5. Other bounds: \\(\\sum_{r=4}^{2n}r^2\\).\\par We will later prove some results using proof by induction.","fontSize":32,"hangingIndent":0,"color":"#151515"}},{"id":"native-2","kind":"sp","box":{"x":812,"y":35,"w":748,"h":889},"fill":"transparent","line":{"color":"transparent"},"transcription":{"tex":"\\textbf{From History of Mathematical Notations, Vol. 2}\\par 438. The sign \\(\\Sigma\\) for summation is due to L. Euler\\(^{1}\\) (1755), who says, “summam indicabimus signo \\(\\Sigma\\).” This symbol was used by Lagrange,\\(^{2}\\) but otherwise received little attention during the eighteenth century. A widely used notation for the sum of a series is the capital letter \\(S\\); it is given, for instance, in G. S. Klügel’s \\(\\text{W\\\"orterbuch.}^{3}\\) The \\(\\Sigma\\) to express “sum” occurs in 1829 in Fourier’s Theory of Heat,\\(^{4}\\) published in 1822, and in C. G. J. Jacobi’s\\(^{5}\\) elliptic functions of 1829.\\par Cauchy\\(^{6}\\) used three indices \\(m,n,r\\), as in \\(\\sum_m^n fr\\). Alfred Pringsheim\\(^{7}\\) marks the sum of an infinite series thus, \\(\\sum_0^\\infty a_r\\).\\par Jahnke\\(^{8}\\) adopts in one place, as a substitute for \\(\\Sigma a_\\nu\\), the simpler sign \\(\\bar a\\), which he borrows from geodesists, to designate the sum \\(a_1+a_2+a_3+\\cdots\\). Additional symbols for sum are given in §410.","fontSize":32,"hangingIndent":0,"color":"#151515"}}],"animation":{"steps":[],"triggers":{},"initialHidden":[]},"deckId":"core-pure-1-c03-full","courseId":"core-pure-1","variant":"full","covers":[],"headerShapes":[],"titlePageCandidate":false,"titlePage":false,"extension":false,"reviewStatus":"source-extracted-not-approved","contentWidth":1600,"contentHeight":964,"measuredLayout":true,"answerOrder":[],"nextActionOrder":[],"afterAnswerActions":{},"sourceMapping":{"method":"Native text and LaTeX conversion; acceptance tracked separately","sourceSlide":2}},{"id":"core-pure-1-c03-full-s003","chapter":3,"sourceSlide":3,"title":"Recap","shapes":[{"id":"native-1","kind":"sp","box":{"x":40,"y":35,"w":1520,"h":50},"fill":"transparent","line":{"color":"transparent"},"transcription":{"tex":"Determine the results by explicitly writing out the terms in each sum.","fontSize":32,"hangingIndent":0,"color":"#151515"}},{"id":"native-2","kind":"sp","box":{"x":40,"y":105,"w":748,